.TH FF 1.SH NAMEff \- find files by name.SH SYNOPSIS\fBff\fRname.SH DESCRIPTION\fBff\fRfinds the files whose names contain\fIname\fRanywhere under the current directory.  It does it by building thecommand\fBdir \-ausr ! grep \-e=\fIname\fRand handing that to a module called\fBshell\fR.\fRBoth are your own OS-9's: load its\fBshell\fRand\fBgrep\fRfirst (\fBload /h1/CMDS/shell /h1/CMDS/grep\fR),because this disk's GNU\fBgrep\fRreads\fB\-e=\fIname\fRas the pattern\fB=\fIname\fRand finds nothing, and with no shell resident nothing runs at all.With nothing to look for it answers in German.\fBfind\fRdoes the same job without a shell..SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOfind, whereis